<BaseLayout title="Privacy — Onuros" description="How Onuros approaches shielded payments, user-controlled disclosure, and development-stage privacy engineering.">
  <section class="page-hero"><p class="kicker">Privacy</p><h1 class="display">Private by default.<br/>Honest by design.</h1><p class="lead">Onuros is developing shielded payments intended to conceal sender, receiver, and amount on-chain, without giving any operator a master viewing key.</p></section>
  <section class="section light"><div class="eyebrow-line"><p class="kicker">The model</p></div><div class="split"><h2 class="section-title">Disclosure stays with the user.</h2><div><p class="copy">A user may choose to disclose a specific payment when proof is needed. That capability does not create a network-wide viewing authority.</p><p class="copy">Privacy reduces public exposure; it does not promise absolute anonymity against every possible observer, device compromise, or operational mistake.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="eyebrow-line"><p class="kicker">Development boundary</p></div><div class="cards"><article class="card"><span class="number">01</span><h3>Shielded transfers</h3><p>Payment data is designed to remain hidden while consensus verifies authorization and value conservation.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">02</span><h3>User-controlled evidence</h3><p>Selective payment disclosure is bound to the exact transaction and chosen output.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">03</span><h3>No production claim</h3><p>The system remains in development-network testing and still requires independent security review before mainnet.</p></article></div><div class="notice">Experimental proof sizes and performance measurements are research results, not guarantees of production privacy or throughput.</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
